d  to california being the largest  incarceration center in the united  states  and the united states  uh having 20 or 30 percent of the  world's inmates  amazing  the politics of law and order  were so successful politically that we  were  creating these huge side effects that  will be with us so i tried a lot of zany  things i hired some extra gang members  on my staff i i i tried to analyze  programs that might  help transform these young people  into  positive and constructive rebels if they  were going to be rebels as opposed to  self-destructive  and i think we had some success i think  we actually changed the minds  on the inside but but everybody thought  i was out of my mind politically that  they did not want to be tagged as soft  on gangs or  using tax dollars to hire ex-gang  members  even if they agreed privately it was for  a worthy purpose so there  i saw the limits of working within the  system  good people  but  calculating about what they had to do to  survive  and excluding certain issues all  together  as  somebody's business down the road but  not the kind of issues they wanted to  take up which meant if you didn't have  outside forces external crises  you might never get the attention of  politicians you had to make it something  that their constituencies cared deeply  about to get them  activated  you wrote a book about gangs and then  you also got involved with ireland  another place with a  history of violence  makes sense doesn't warfare  uh  your your ireland book is one of my  favorites well i got very interested in  ireland  because in the late 60s  after chicago i felt like they'd had a  lot of my american identity beaten out  of me  and i had never been raised as an irish  american  i was a i was a white young man  from the midwest and  and as the uh  identity got taken away the women took  their identity the blacks took their  identity the chicanos took theirs  what was left was uh being a white man  yeah which pretty boring  yeah it'