ion  uh temporarily but they stopped it from  functioning stopped it from approving  all of these onerous uh regulations  when we tried to stop segregated lunch  counters  30 years before we we did but then they  would reopen and it would be a long long  process  but in this case i thought  you know when the when the the dust  cleared and the smoke settled and the  tear gas was gone  people would look at it more clearly and  see that  that a more secretive apparatus the wto  had been exposed  and we have bought precious time for the  american people and american  institutions to consider what what is  our role in the world you know are we  trying to create a corporate empire  are we trying to roll back uh  protections on health safety environment  consumer products that were the  achievements of past generations and i  think it was very healthy the seattle  generation i'm afraid  became derailed  somewhat because of iraq so we won't  know  what the seattle generation  would have become  for that uh  brief period though it was extremely  powerful it was global  and it's still around but until iraq has  really resolved i don't think you'll  find  enough attention here  the evidence that it goes on is look at  latin america eight nine ten elections  uh to the to the presidency in countries  that used to be under dictatorships that  were considered  economic satellites of the united states  whether it's  argentina brazil venezuela uruguay  paraguay  chile the list goes on these are all  countries that have turned against  what was transpiring in seattle so it's  by no means over and i i went to many of  these places i went to the  demonstrations in miami i went to cancun  to the next effort at the wto  i went to bolivia on a couple of  occasions to investigate the rise of  a peasant movement in the election of  the first indigenous  president of that country  all very moving and interesting  experiences that reminded me of  mississippi in the beginning  but now the sout